WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
Launch is a decision you make with evidence.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:10.115
By now your system streams, retries, routes models by cost, emits traces, and sits behind guards you’ve measured.

3
00:00:10.395 --> 00:00:14.491
None of that answers the question your stakeholders will ask: is it ready?

4
00:00:14.771 --> 00:00:27.336
Google’s SRE book includes a launch coordination checklist that covers architecture, capacity, failure and failover, monitoring, security, automation, external dependencies, and rollout planning.

5
00:00:27.616 --> 00:00:37.301
Its value isn’t the specific questions; it’s the habit of answering each one with evidence before launch, in writing, so the launch is a decision rather than a hope.

6
00:00:37.581 --> 00:00:42.275
LLM features need that checklist plus a few items specific to models.

7
00:00:42.555 --> 00:00:47.717
The output is probabilistic, so “it works” means “the evals pass at a stated rate.

8
00:00:47.997 --> 00:00:55.037
” The failures are often semantic, so an error-rate dashboard can be green while the feature says something harmful or wrong.

9
00:00:55.317 --> 00:01:02.677
A third party controls a critical dependency (the model), and it can change behavior, refuse, or be deprecated.

10
00:01:02.957 --> 00:01:06.989
And costs scale with usage in a way that can surprise you within hours.

11
00:01:07.269 --> 00:01:10.363
The checklist below is what this studio has built toward.

12
00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:13.011
Each item names the evidence you attach.

13
00:01:13.291 --> 00:01:16.277
If you can’t attach the evidence, the item isn’t done.
